WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.770
Language learning starts with you,
with the person, with the learner.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.240
Just do it!

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.380
Language learning to me is almost
like a model of life itself.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.560
So many things can happen
to us in our lives.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.580
What matters is what we do.
And when we start something,

00:00:17.585 --> 00:00:19.260
that's the most important step.

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:23.815
To do something! And if we do
something, it may trigger a series

00:00:23.815 --> 00:00:25.945
of events that we didn't anticipate.

00:00:26.095 --> 00:00:29.634
Some might be better than we hoped
for, some might be worse than we

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.085
hoped for. But throughout my life,
every time I have undertaken to

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.425
do something, it has brought me

00:00:35.465 --> 00:00:38.195
some of the greatest
opportunities in my life.

00:00:38.195 --> 00:00:41.015
When I was, I think, 19,
working on a construction

00:00:41.015 --> 00:00:42.965
site in Montreal, I was fired.

00:00:43.025 --> 00:00:47.615
That particular section of the job had
been completed, so I went down to the

00:00:47.615 --> 00:00:51.695
dock in Montreal, and for three days, I asked
to see the captain of the various ships

00:00:51.695 --> 00:00:53.135
that were docked in Montreal Port.

00:00:53.195 --> 00:00:55.055
I asked to work my way across to Europe.

00:00:55.085 --> 00:00:59.465
Day three, I found work on a German
freighter called the Gerda Schnell.

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:02.435
And here you see a picture of
me with my crew members.

00:01:02.435 --> 00:01:04.175
And I worked my way across to Europe.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.390
I ended up in Grenoble in France.
And here is another situation.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:12.980
I had a very good friend who was a Turkish
student whose name was Cağpar, and

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.600
I was studying English and French.

00:01:14.605 --> 00:01:17.750
I didn't know what to study, but he
was studying political science.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.800
So I switched over to political science
and the following year I went to

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:28.070
Sciences Po (Institut d'études politiques) in Paris, and got a
scholarship from the French government.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.250
Eventually I wrote my foreign
service exam there for the Canadian

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.780
Diplomatic Service and was accepted.

00:01:32.845 --> 00:01:38.635
So all of these things came from
my decision to go and hitchhike

00:01:38.635 --> 00:01:39.865
on a boat across to Europe.

00:01:40.285 --> 00:01:44.035
Similarly, when I was with the
diplomatic service, I heard that they

00:01:44.035 --> 00:01:48.175
were looking for someone to study
Mandarin Chinese, because in 1968,

00:01:48.535 --> 00:01:51.595
Canada was getting ready to recognize
the People's Republic of China.

00:01:51.595 --> 00:01:54.085
They wanted to train some
people up in Chinese.

00:01:54.145 --> 00:01:56.965
So I went and started
taking Chinese lessons.

00:01:57.085 --> 00:01:58.075
I didn't learn very much.

00:01:58.315 --> 00:02:01.615
But I told my boss, "Hey,
I am learning Chinese.

00:02:01.675 --> 00:02:02.515
I'm your man."

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.135
Why wouldn't they choose me?

00:02:04.255 --> 00:02:08.035
Next thing you know, I'm in Hong
Kong studying Chinese, prowling the

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.995
bookstores for material, reading and
listening and doing whatever I can.

00:02:11.995 --> 00:02:12.055
I.

00:02:12.614 --> 00:02:16.755
I mean, I can go on and on and on in
my professional career about things that

00:02:16.755 --> 00:02:21.765
I did, whether it be in my diplomatic
career or in my career, first as a

00:02:21.765 --> 00:02:25.875
forest industry executive, and then an
entrepreneur in the forest industry.

00:02:25.904 --> 00:02:30.195
Very often it's not what I expected would
happen that ended up happening. But it

00:02:30.195 --> 00:02:34.755
was the fact that I did something that
caused opportunities to come my way.

00:02:35.114 --> 00:02:36.644
You know, it's a bit like the book that I

00:02:36.975 --> 00:02:40.335
listened to and read in
Danish, "Live More, Think Less"

00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:42.675
We are bombarded by thoughts all day long.

00:02:42.675 --> 00:02:44.235
50,000 thoughts

00:02:44.235 --> 00:02:44.685
apparently.

00:02:44.685 --> 00:02:46.095
Some are good, some are bad.

00:02:46.305 --> 00:02:50.295
Some people dwell on the negative thoughts
and some of them end up with depression.

00:02:50.325 --> 00:02:53.355
It's how we choose to
deal with these thoughts.

00:02:53.355 --> 00:02:57.375
It's our belief system that determines
how we cope with these thoughts.

00:02:57.435 --> 00:02:59.655
And the same is true in language learning.

00:02:59.835 --> 00:03:04.725
You know, you may not be tremendously
motivated to learn Spanish or Japanese,

00:03:04.755 --> 00:03:08.775
and you start on it, and you may find
something there that motivates you to

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:13.665
continue. Or you may meet people through
having learned or studied that language.

00:03:13.695 --> 00:03:16.575
It's just as possible that
you will be motivated then

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.075
to move to another language.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.105
In other words, that initial
impetus to learn language

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.580
might lead you to learn language.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:29.100
The important thing is to do something
to get started. As the French say, "l'appétit vient en mangeant"

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.650
So there are so many examples of
this in my life, but I thought

00:03:34.655 --> 00:03:36.420
of it with specific reference to

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:37.470
language learning.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.630
Many people say, "Well, I have no talent.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.250
I was never any good."

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.950
Well, maybe you should
just give it a chance.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.109
Push on it a little bit, see what happens.

00:03:46.109 --> 00:03:47.130
Push on that door.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.839
It may open, it may not. But maybe in
trying to learn this language, you'll

00:03:51.839 --> 00:03:55.620
meet some people or you'll find another
language that interests you. Or even

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.540
something else unrelated to language.

00:03:57.839 --> 00:04:01.345
But the main thing is, everything
that happens to us in our lives,

00:04:01.405 --> 00:04:03.054
we're surrounded by opportunities.

00:04:03.054 --> 00:04:07.345
But what matters is whether we do
something. And the more active we are,

00:04:07.405 --> 00:04:11.575
the greater number of these opportunities
will come our way. Including the fact

00:04:11.575 --> 00:04:15.355
that the more languages we know, the
greater the number of opportunities

00:04:15.595 --> 00:04:16.765
that are going to come our way.

00:04:16.855 --> 00:04:20.214
So I just wanted to talk a little
more generally about the importance

00:04:20.274 --> 00:04:22.525
of being proactive in our lives.

00:04:22.615 --> 00:04:27.115
"Do it!" as that Nike
advertising slogan went.

00:04:27.235 --> 00:04:27.985
Thanks for listening.

